WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:01.793
- [Instructor] So, what
we're gonna think about

00:00:01.793 --> 00:00:04.890
in this video is multiplying fractions.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.870
So, let's say that we wanted to take 2/3

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:12.150
and we want to multiply it by four,

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.920
what is this going to be equal to?

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.320
Pause this video and try to
think about it on your own.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.020
Alright, now let's work
through this together.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.480
And, to help us, I will use a number line,

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.902
and let's say that each
of these hash marks

00:00:25.902 --> 00:00:27.240
represent a third.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.240
So, this is zero, this is 1/3, 2/3, 3/3,

00:00:32.971 --> 00:00:37.971
4/3, 5/3, 6/3, 7/3,

00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:44.138
8/3, and 9/3, and so
where is 2/3 times one?

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.650
Well, 2/3 times one is
just going to be 2/3,

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:53.290
we just take a jump of
2/3, so that is times 1.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:56.250
If we multiply by, or if
we take 2/3 times two,

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:57.330
that'll be two jumps,

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:02.330
so one 2/3, two 2/3, three
2/3, and then four 2/3.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:10.100
So, we just took four jumps of 2/3 each.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:15.100
You could view that as 2/3
plus 2/3 plus 2/3 plus 2/3,

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.510
and where does that get us to?

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.890
It got us to 8/3.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:28.890
So, notice, 2/3 times
four is equal to 8/3.

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:30.110
Now, we could go the other way,

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.520
we could look at a number line

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.640
and think about what are ways to represent

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.090
what the number line is showing us?

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.080
And, on Khan Academy, we
have some example problems

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:37.913
that do it that way,

00:01:37.913 --> 00:01:39.890
so I thought it would be good
to do an example like that.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:41.620
And, so, let's label this number line

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:42.453
a little bit different.

00:01:42.453 --> 00:01:44.840
Instead of each of these
lines representing a third,

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.740
let's say they represent a half,

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:51.740
so zero, 1/2, 2/2, 3/2, 4/2, 5/2,

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.270
why did I write 5/6, my
brain is going ahead,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:02.270
5/2, 6/2, 7/2, 8/2, and 9/2.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:08.420
And, let's say we were to
see something like this.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.804
So, if you were to just
see this representation,

00:02:10.804 --> 00:02:14.990
so I'm going to try to draw it like this,

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:17.740
so if you were to just
see this representation,

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:19.920
what is that trying to represent?

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.138
What type of multiplication
is that trying to represent?

00:02:24.138 --> 00:02:28.646
Well, you could view that
as 3/2 plus another 3/2

00:02:28.646 --> 00:02:30.188
plus another 3/2, 'cause, notice,

00:02:30.188 --> 00:02:34.430
each of these jumps are three 1/2, or 3/2.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:39.430
So, you could view this
as 3/2 plus 3/2 plus 3/2,

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.380
or another way of thinking about it

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:45.110
is this is three jumps of 3/2.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.580
So, you can also view this
as doing the same thing

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:52.340
as three times 3/2, and
what are these equal to?

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.726
Well, 3/2 plus 3/2 plus
3/2, or three times 3/2,

00:02:55.726 --> 00:02:59.033
it gets you to 9/2.

